 The currency symbol is stated by the panel, the card header, or the topmost axis tick — not repeated
 down every row or tick. Repeating a currency code five times is five copies of a fact the reader
 learned from the first one.
 
+**M8. `MoneyText` is the only place a decimal becomes text.**
+A surface renders an amount with `<MoneyText>`, which formats nothing itself — it resolves the value
+once through `moneyParts()` and renders the parts. The currency is a prefix in its own element with a
+token gap, never concatenated onto the digits.
+*Fails if:* any component other than `lib/money.ts` calls `Number`, `toFixed` or `Intl.NumberFormat`
+on an amount. Checked over `components/**` by `tests/design_contract_test.ts`.
+
+**M9. A hero amount is not coloured by direction; a movement is.**
+An amount that states a balance reads in the surface's own foreground — in the reference cards the
+hero number is plain and only the delta beside it is green. An amount that states a movement passes
+`tone='direction'` (or is a `DeltaChip`) and takes `--ns-money-*`.
+*Fails if:* a hero metric is rendered in green because it is positive.
+
 ## Status and lifecycle
 
 **S1. Status presentation has one source of truth.**
 `lib/ui/status.ts` owns every label and tone. Nothing at a call site decides a status colour.

